\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces The 4-cycle and Simpson. (a) Faces of the marginal polytope $\mathcal  D$ by dimension, against the dimension of the fibre over their interior. The Clauser--Horne facets are simplices, so there the data determine the joint law; positivity facets leave a four-dimensional fibre. (b) The symmetric CHSH path: the identified interval of the never-observed $P(A_0=0,A_1=0)$ closes at $S=2$, where every equivariant section is forced to $1/4$, and past it the data are contextual. (c) Straight paths to three kinds of face: the two sections disagree until the fibre closes, and only a CH facet closes it. (d) A Simpson reversal made by selection: positive association within each slice of $X_1$ and negative overall. Markov gluing has zero association within the slices, and the data allow either sign overall.}}{68}{figure.21}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cycle}{{21}{68}{The 4-cycle and Simpson. (a) Faces of the marginal polytope $\mathcal D$ by dimension, against the dimension of the fibre over their interior. The Clauser--Horne facets are simplices, so there the data determine the joint law; positivity facets leave a four-dimensional fibre. (b) The symmetric CHSH path: the identified interval of the never-observed $P(A_0=0,A_1=0)$ closes at $S=2$, where every equivariant section is forced to $1/4$, and past it the data are contextual. (c) Straight paths to three kinds of face: the two sections disagree until the fibre closes, and only a CH facet closes it. (d) A Simpson reversal made by selection: positive association within each slice of $X_1$ and negative overall. Markov gluing has zero association within the slices, and the data allow either sign overall}{figure.21}{}}
