\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces The kinds of undetectable edit, and one change read as a sum of them. Each diagram shows the four tables of the CHSH cover on the sides of the cycle $A_0$--$B_0$--$A_1$--$B_1$ (rows: the $A$ variable; columns: the $B$ variable). A $+$ cell gains mass and a $-$ cell loses it. (a) A swap: four cells in one table, all margins fixed. (b) A margin shift: one single margin moves, by two cells in each of the two tables that contain it. (c)--(e) Circuits of $6$, $8$ and $9$ cells that coordinate three or four tables. (f) A detectable edit: one table alone, so the tables disagree on a shared margin. (g) The poisoned step of \cref  {ex:poisonstream}, cell by cell. (h) Its conformal decomposition beside a clean step's: clean drift is a sum of $4$-cell edits, the poisoned step needs coordinated ones. (i) The coordination (the least share any decomposition puts on circuits of more than $4$ cells) of every step.}}{74}{figure.22}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:edittypes}{{22}{74}{The kinds of undetectable edit, and one change read as a sum of them. Each diagram shows the four tables of the CHSH cover on the sides of the cycle $A_0$--$B_0$--$A_1$--$B_1$ (rows: the $A$ variable; columns: the $B$ variable). A $+$ cell gains mass and a $-$ cell loses it. (a) A swap: four cells in one table, all margins fixed. (b) A margin shift: one single margin moves, by two cells in each of the two tables that contain it. (c)--(e) Circuits of $6$, $8$ and $9$ cells that coordinate three or four tables. (f) A detectable edit: one table alone, so the tables disagree on a shared margin. (g) The poisoned step of \cref {ex:poisonstream}, cell by cell. (h) Its conformal decomposition beside a clean step's: clean drift is a sum of $4$-cell edits, the poisoned step needs coordinated ones. (i) The coordination (the least share any decomposition puts on circuits of more than $4$ cells) of every step}{figure.22}{}}
\newlabel{fig:edittypes@cref}{{[figure][22][]22}{[1][73][]74}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces How little poisoning. (a) Undetectable placements (signed circuits that keep the data in $\mathcal  D$) against the face dimension of the data, by fibre dimension, over all $2721$ faces of the 4-cycle. The count is monotone in the hidden structure, and $\dim G+\dim (\text  {fibre})=|S|-1$ splits that structure into room for the data and room for the rule. (b) The cheapest undetectable flip (gap $0.01$) is usually one circuit of $4$ cells, for every $n$ up to $8$. (c) One poisoned batch at $t=10$, after the hidden structure changed at $t=6$. The identified interval (shaded) flips below the threshold for one step; the maximum-entropy rule stays flipped for six. (d) Removing one batch at a time at the flipped times: only the poisoned batch reverts the decision.}}{75}{figure.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:poisonflip}{{23}{75}{How little poisoning. (a) Undetectable placements (signed circuits that keep the data in $\mathcal D$) against the face dimension of the data, by fibre dimension, over all $2721$ faces of the 4-cycle. The count is monotone in the hidden structure, and $\dim G+\dim (\text {fibre})=|S|-1$ splits that structure into room for the data and room for the rule. (b) The cheapest undetectable flip (gap $0.01$) is usually one circuit of $4$ cells, for every $n$ up to $8$. (c) One poisoned batch at $t=10$, after the hidden structure changed at $t=6$. The identified interval (shaded) flips below the threshold for one step; the maximum-entropy rule stays flipped for six. (d) Removing one batch at a time at the flipped times: only the poisoned batch reverts the decision}{figure.23}{}}
\newlabel{fig:poisonflip@cref}{{[figure][23][]23}{[1][73][]75}}
